## Step 3 — WAVE 3: Reference Indexing

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Reference Indexer (REFS)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

REFS_ADDED   : [<all ./.harness/ref/path values from WAVE 1 RETURN blocks>]
OUTPUT FILE  : ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md
PROMPT_PATH  : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

TASK
────
1. Read ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md completely. Parse existing Summary block and flat table.

2. Classify each path in REFS_ADDED:
     .git/ present → repo
     *.pdf, *.txt, *.zip, *.xml → doc
     URL not cloned → ref

3. For each path, read the row Ci agents appended to recover URL, description, node.
   Do NOT scan ./.harness/ref/ recursively — only process paths in REFS_ADDED.

4. Append new rows (skip if Name already present, case-insensitive):
   repo : | [<name>](./<name>) | repo | <url> | <description> | [<CURRENT_NAME>](../.harness) |
   ref  : | <name>             | ref  | <url> | <description> | [<CURRENT_NAME>](../.harness) |
   doc  : | [<file>](./<file>) | doc  | <url_or_local> | <description> | [<CURRENT_NAME>](../.harness) |

5. Update the guideline comment block at the top of OUTPUT FILE:
     LAST UPDATED : <ISO-8601 date>
     Total repo   : <n>
     Total ref    : <n>
     Total doc    : <n>

OUTPUT FILE must be written before returning.

RETURN:
  STATUS        : done | failed
  OUTPUT_FILE   : ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md
  REPO_COUNT    : <n>
  REF_COUNT     : <n>
  DOC_COUNT     : <n>
  DUPES_REMOVED : <n>
  FAILURE_REASON: <only if failed>

RETRY: coordinator re-spawns once on failure.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**Gate:** wait for REFS. If fails after retry: pass null ref index to WAVE 4, note failure. Do not halt.

---

## Step 4 — WAVE 4: Research Thread Synthesis

After WAVE 3 completes, coordinator reads all RCi outputs and derives M research thread
slugs. Numbers them 01, 02, ..., 0M. Appends RS1..RSm nodes to STATE_FILE. Spawns all
RSi agents in parallel. Each RSi writes one file to ./.harness/researchs/.

Thread derivation: group WAVE 1+2 findings by major theme. Each distinct theme = one
thread. Minimum 1. Maximum = C_NODE_COUNT. Slug = kebab-case ≤ 4 words per theme.

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Research Thread Synthesis (RSi)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

THREAD_SLUG      : <slug-i — derived from review outputs>
THREAD_INDEX     : <zero-padded: 01, 02, ...>
EXPLORATION FILES: [Ci outputs that contribute to this thread]
REVIEW FILES     : [RCi outputs that contribute to this thread]
REFS INDEX       : ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md   (or null)
EXISTING_FILE    : ./.harness/researchs/<THREAD_INDEX>-<THREAD_SLUG>.md
                   (read first if exists — do NOT overwrite blindly)
OUTPUT FILE      : ./.harness/researchs/<THREAD_INDEX>-<THREAD_SLUG>.md
PROMPT_PATH      : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

TASK
────
1. If EXISTING_FILE exists on disk: read it completely before anything else.
   It is prior validated research for this thread. Do not re-derive it. Do not erase it.

2. Read every assigned EXPLORATION FILE completely.

3. Read every assigned REVIEW FILE completely.
   For every DISPUTED claim: discard the original, use the corrected version.
   For every CONFIDENCE=LOW review: treat its paired exploration with reduced trust.

4. If REFS INDEX non-null: read it for source context.

5. Synthesize findings for this thread into OUTPUT FILE:

   # <CURRENT_NAME> — <THREAD_SLUG>

   ## Synopsis
   <3–5 sentences: core problem for this thread, key findings, critical unknowns>

   ## Findings
   <Known (with citations), Unknown, Contested (corrected).
    Depth and precision. Symbol names, file paths, line numbers.>

   ## Open Questions
   <Numbered list. Each: question, why it matters, what evidence exists.>

   ## Known Gaps
   <What is missing for this thread's scope. Specific crates + source locations.>

   ## Constraints
   <Hard constraints relevant to this thread.>

   ## Sources
   | Claim / Finding | Source Name | ./.harness/ref/ path | Node |
   <One row per citation. Node = the Ci that introduced the source.
    Every claim marked "Known" must have a row here.
    Sources not in ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md: list inline — no ./.harness/ref/ path.>

   If EXISTING_FILE had content: preserve in correct sections. Mark updates
   clearly. Delete a prior finding only if DISPUTED and corrected by authoritative source.

OUTPUT FILE must be written before returning.

RETURN:
  STATUS              : done | failed
  NODE_ID             : <RSi>
  OUTPUT_FILE         : ./.harness/researchs/<THREAD_INDEX>-<THREAD_SLUG>.md
  THREAD_SLUG         : <slug>
  THREAD_INDEX        : <NN>
  FINDING_COUNT       : <n>
  OPEN_QUESTION_COUNT : <n>
  GAP_COUNT           : <n>
  PRIOR_PRESERVED     : true | false
  FAILURE_REASON      : <only if failed>

RETRY: coordinator re-spawns once on failure.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**Gate:** collect all m RSi returns. Re-spawn failed once. If all RSi fail after retry: HALT.

---

## Step 5 — WAVE 5: Design Synthesis

Spawn one design agent. ONLY input is the researchs/ directory.

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Design Synthesis (DESIGN_SYNTH)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

RESEARCH FILES  : [./.harness/researchs/01-<slug>.md, ..., ./.harness/researchs/0m-<slug>.md]
                  (all RSi outputs in index order; m = WAVE 4 thread count)
EXISTING_DESIGN : ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md   (read first if exists — do NOT overwrite blindly)
OUTPUT FILE     : ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md
PROMPT_PATH     : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

STRICT INPUT BOUNDARY: Read ONLY RESEARCH FILES and EXISTING_DESIGN (if present).
Do not access ./.harness/scratchs/, ./.harness/ref/INDEX.md, or the project codebase directly.

TASK
────
1. If EXISTING_DESIGN exists on disk: read it completely before anything else.
   It is prior validated design. Do not re-derive it from scratch. Do not erase it.

2. Read all RESEARCH FILES completely and deeply. Every section. Every finding.
   Every open question. Every gap. Every constraint. All threads.

3. If EXISTING_DESIGN exists: measure anchor coverage before writing anything.
   For each section in EXISTING_DESIGN, check whether its stated research anchor
   still exists across RESEARCH FILES (same finding, same constraint, same gap).
   Classify:
     ANCHORED   — anchor present and unchanged across RESEARCH FILES
     UPDATED    — anchor present but finding has changed; section needs revision
     ORPHANED   — anchor absent from all RESEARCH FILES; prior decision is invalidated

   Compute: orphaned_ratio = ORPHANED / total_sections

   If orphaned_ratio ≥ 0.5:
     Full divergence — EXISTING_DESIGN is built on a different research foundation.
     Discard it entirely. Re-derive OUTPUT FILE fresh from RESEARCH FILES only.
     Set MODE=replaced.

   If orphaned_ratio < 0.5:
     Partial re-synthesis — preserve ANCHORED sections, revise UPDATED sections,
     delete ORPHANED sections, derive new sections from RESEARCH FILES findings
     not yet covered. Every surviving decision must still trace to RESEARCH FILES.
     Set MODE=re-synthesized.

4. Write OUTPUT FILE:

   # <CURRENT_NAME> — Design

   ## 1. Architecture
   <Complete request flow as transformation chain. Each segment: primary responsibility,
    protocol boundary, type that crosses the boundary.>

   ## 2. A-Segment: Ingress
   <Transport, authentication, admission. Entry State, Transformation, Exit State.
    Symbol-level detail.>

   ## 3. B-Segment: Gateway
   <Public-facing bridge. New/modified crates. Primary types and traits.
    Request/response lifecycle.>

   ## 4. C-Segment: Mesh
   <How requests land on the right actor. Placement, lifecycle, reconciliation.
    What is unchanged vs what must change.>

   ## 5. Agent Spawning: <N> Resolution
   <Concrete recommendation. Rationale. Rejected alternatives with reasons.>

   ## 6. Capability and Security Model
   <Token lifecycle, delegation chain, enforcement points. What is missing today.>

   ## 7. Crate Plan
   if PROJECT_TYPE=existing  → | Crate | Action (create|modify) | Change Summary   | Design Rationale |
   if PROJECT_TYPE=greenfield → | Crate | Action (create)        | Responsibility   | Design Rationale |
   After writing §7: if TOOLCHAIN=unknown, derive TOOLCHAIN from the crates listed here
   (Cargo.toml crates → cargo; go modules → go; npm packages → npm). Bind for WAVE 8.

   ## 8. Functional Requirements
   | FR-id | Statement | Segment | Status (addressed|gap) | Design Section |
   <From Constraints and Known Gaps across all RESEARCH FILES. Every gap becomes a requirement.
    No gap without an addressing section or explicit deferral with rationale.>

   ## 9. Invariants
   Before writing, run a proof-structure audit across all candidates:
     COVERAGE    : every gap across RESEARCH FILES §Known Gaps has ≥1 invariant;
                   any unaddressed gap → derive and add an invariant
     DUPLICATION : no two invariants cover the same domain claim;
                   if one subsumes another → drop the weaker
     DERIVATION  : every invariant traces to exactly one DESIGN_THINKING source:
                     §1 shape correctness | §2 call graph node | §4 error mode |
                     §5 R dependency | §6 boundary | §8 resource scope
     PROVABILITY : statement is in ∀ x. P(x) → Q(x) form, expressible as a Bend law
     SECURITY    : invariants that guard trust boundaries or capability transitions
                   are tagged SEC; SEC-tagged invariants are required FV nodes
                   regardless of total FV_NODE_COUNT
   Each entry: slug (kebab-case), statement, DESIGN_THINKING source, SEC (if applicable).
   Minimum 3. Maximum 12.

   ## 10. Open Questions
   <From RESEARCH FILES: which remain unresolved, which resolved (how), which deferred.>

OUTPUT FILE must be written before returning.

RETURN:
  STATUS           : done | failed
  OUTPUT_FILE      : ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md
  MODE             : created | re-synthesized | replaced
  INVARIANT_COUNT  : <n>
  FR_COUNT         : <n total>
  FR_GAP_COUNT     : <n with status=gap>
  FAILURE_REASON   : <only if failed>

RETRY: coordinator re-spawns once on failure.
On second failure: coordinator HALTS — WAVE 6 cannot proceed without designs/01-system-overview.md.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**Gate:** wait for DESIGN_SYNTH. Extract INVARIANT_COUNT → FV_NODE_COUNT. If 0 or designs/01-system-overview.md absent: FV sub-wave skipped.

---

## Step 6 — WAVE 6: Formal Verification + Application Design

Read ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md. Extract §9 Invariants: m entries with slug + statement.
Spawn m FVi agents in parallel (or skip if m=0). After all FVi gate: spawn APP_DESIGN.

### Sub-wave 6A — Formal Verification (m agents, parallel)

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Provness Node FVi
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

INVARIANT_SLUG  : <slug-i from designs/01-system-overview.md §9>
INVARIANT_STMT  : <statement-i from designs/01-system-overview.md §9>
DESIGN_FILE     : ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md
OUTPUT_DIR      : ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/
PROMPT_PATH     : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

STRICT INPUT BOUNDARY: Read ONLY DESIGN_FILE. Do not read researchs/*.md or ./.harness/scratchs/.

TASK
────
1. Read DESIGN_FILE §9. Find INVARIANT_SLUG. Read statement and derivation.
2. Create OUTPUT_DIR if absent.
3. Write LAWS.bend: declare invariant as Bend law (axiom, no implementation).
4. Write PROOF.bend: implement proof satisfying LAWS.bend.
   Only types derivable from INVARIANT_STMT. No external imports.
5. Write main.bend: entry point running proof against law.
6. Run: BENDTT=<BENDTT> bend --verdict ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/main.bend
   Classify: VERIFIED (exit 0) | UNVERIFIED (non-zero) | COUNTEREXAMPLE (produces counterexample)
7. Write ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/result.md:
   # <slug-i>
   ## Verdict: VERIFIED | UNVERIFIED | COUNTEREXAMPLE
   ## Invariant
   <statement>
   ## Output
   <bend --verdict output verbatim>

OUTPUT_DIR files must be written before returning.

RETURN:
  STATUS         : done | failed
  SLUG           : <slug-i>
  OUTPUT_DIR     : ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/
  VERDICT        : VERIFIED | UNVERIFIED | COUNTEREXAMPLE
  FAILURE_REASON : <only if failed>

RETRY: coordinator re-spawns once on failure.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**Gate:** collect all m FVi returns. Re-spawn failed once. Proceed to APP_DESIGN.

### Sub-wave 6B — Application Design Synthesis

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Application Design Synthesis (APP_DESIGN)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

DESIGN_FILE      : ./.harness/designs/01-system-overview.md
FV_RESULTS       : [{ slug: <slug-i>, verdict: VERIFIED|UNVERIFIED|COUNTEREXAMPLE,
                      output_dir: ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/ }, ...]
                   (empty if FV sub-wave skipped)
DESIGN_THINKING  : <DESIGN_THINKING from State Model>
OUTPUT FILE      : ./.harness/designs/02-app-architecture.md
PROMPT_PATH      : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

STRICT INPUT BOUNDARY: Read ONLY DESIGN_FILE, FV result.md files, and DESIGN_THINKING.
Do not read researchs/*.md or any ./.harness/scratchs/ file.

TASK
────
1. Read DESIGN_FILE completely and deeply.

2. Proof Assembly — build a verification matrix before touching the domain model.
   For each entry in FV_RESULTS: read ./.harness/provness/<slug-i>/result.md.
   Classify each verdict and extract its structural implication:

   VERIFIED       → extract: which DESIGN_THINKING section it secures (§1/§2/§4/§5/§6/§8);
                    what duplication it eliminates in the domain model;
                    whether it proves .pipe() composability is safe (§7)
   COUNTEREXAMPLE → SECURITY FINDING: the domain has a falsifiable assumption at this invariant.
                    Derive: the specific required domain model revision; which §Boundary Schema
                    entry is wrong or missing; record in §Security Findings.
                    Not suppressible — revise the domain model before writing §Domain Model.
   UNVERIFIED     → PROOF GAP: the invariant could not be proven; add to §Functional Requirements
                    Coverage with status=PROOF_GAP. Treat as an open design gap.

   Assembly result: { slug → verdict, dt_source, implication, security_flag }
   This matrix drives ALL subsequent sections — shapes carry verified proofs, call graph
   nodes carry pre/post conditions, error topology derives from COUNTEREXAMPLE falsifications,
   boundary schema derives from VERIFIED boundary proofs.

3. Read DESIGN_THINKING completely.

<thinking>
Apply DESIGN_THINKING as your thinking framework using the Proof Assembly matrix as evidence:
  §1  Shapes: annotate each type with which VERIFIED invariants guarantee its correctness.
              A shape whose correctness invariant is COUNTEREXAMPLE must be revised first.
              A shape with no invariant coverage is a hole — derive and add one.
  §2  Happy path: each call graph node carries its VERIFIED pre/post conditions.
                  A node whose invariant is UNVERIFIED has an unproven transition — flag it.
  §3  Cardinality: one-shot or Stream per node, unchanged.
  §4  Break points: COUNTEREXAMPLE falsifications reveal error modes the design did not model.
                    Every COUNTEREXAMPLE maps to at least one new or revised E-channel entry.
  §5  R channel: VERIFIED invariants ARE compile-time proofs — each R entry is a verified claim.
                 UNVERIFIED invariant → R entry is unproven; mark as PROOF_GAP in §Dependency Surface.
  §6  Boundary: trust boundaries hold EXACTLY where VERIFIED boundary invariants hold.
                A COUNTEREXAMPLE on a boundary invariant means the boundary is wrong — revise.
                Trust nothing the proofs have not verified.
  §7  Composability: .pipe() layers are safe iff they do not violate VERIFIED invariants.
                     Prove composability: each behavior layer preserves the verified call graph shape.
  §8  Resource scope: acquire/release correctness is a VERIFIED invariant.
  §9  R-swap: the graph is correct under test layers iff VERIFIED invariants hold for both
              production R and test R — same proof, different layer.
  §10 gen = A (proven happy path nodes), pipe = E (error enumeration proven from §4).
No COUNTEREXAMPLE result may be suppressed. No UNVERIFIED invariant may be ignored.
</thinking>

4. Write OUTPUT FILE — the final domain breakdown. WAVE 7 reads ONLY this file.
   Everything needed to synthesize the implementation coordinator must be here.

   # <CURRENT_NAME> — Application Design

   ## Domain Model
   <§1 Shapes: every record, ID type, variant, error type. Rust type signatures.
    Shapes only — no implementations.>

   ## Call Graph
   <§2 Happy path. Each node: function name, input type, output type. Cardinality per node.>

   ## Error Topology
   <§4 Break points per node. Error type. Strategy (retry|escape|die).
    E-channel scoping per layer.>

   ## Dependency Surface
   <§5 R entries per node. What can be swapped for tests (§9).>

   ## Boundary Schema
   <§6 Every untrusted→trusted conversion point. What validates what.>

   ## Behavior Layers
   <§7 Cross-cutting concerns: retry, timeout, tracing, auth.
    What wraps which nodes. Core graph stays clean.>

   ## Resource Scope
   <§8 Acquire/release topology: connections, handles, sessions.>

   ## Crate Implementation Plan
   | Crate | Action | Primary Types | Call Graph Nodes | Error Types Scoped | R Requirements |
   <From DESIGN_FILE §7. One row per crate. Complete enough for an impl agent to act on.>

   ## Functional Requirements Coverage
   | FR-id | Statement | Crate(s) | Specific Change | Closes Gap |
   <Every FR with status=gap from DESIGN_FILE §8 must appear here.
    FR_UNCOVERED must be empty for STATUS=done.>

   ## Verified Invariants
   <VERIFIED slugs only. Each: slug, statement, implication for implementation.>
   <COUNTEREXAMPLE slugs: excluded — explain what the falsification revealed and
    what domain claim was revised in response.>

   ## Surface Inventory
   | Surface | Type | User / Consumer | Production-Required | Rationale |
   <Enumerate every surface the system must expose to be production-usable and deployable.
    Type: cli | web | api | agent | tui | (other — derive from domain).
    Production-Required: yes | no (yes = system is not deployable without it).
    Each row becomes one Si node in WAVE 6C. Minimum 1 row.
    Derive from domain entry points and §Call Graph — who calls what, from where?>

OUTPUT FILE must be written before returning.

RETURN:
  STATUS              : done | failed
  OUTPUT_FILE         : ./.harness/designs/02-app-architecture.md
  CRATE_COUNT         : <n>
  FR_COVERED          : <n gap FRs addressed>
  FR_UNCOVERED        : [<FR-ids unaddressed — must be empty for STATUS=done>]
  VERIFIED_INVARIANTS : <n>
  COUNTEREXAMPLES     : <n>
  SURFACE_COUNT       : <s — number of rows in §Surface Inventory>
  SURFACE_TYPES       : [<type1>, <type2>, ...]
  FAILURE_REASON      : <only if failed>

RETRY: coordinator re-spawns once on failure.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**Gate:** wait for APP_DESIGN. Read designs/02-app-architecture.md §Surface Inventory → derive S_NODE_COUNT and surface types. Append Si nodes to STATE_FILE. Spawn all Si in parallel.

### Sub-wave 6C — Surface Design Synthesis (dynamic)

S_NODE_COUNT and surface types are resolved from designs/02-app-architecture.md §Surface Inventory.
Spawn all Si agents in parallel — one per surface type.

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
AGENT SUBGRAPH — Surface Design Node Si
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

NODE_ID          : <Si>
SURFACE_TYPE     : <type — cli | web | api | agent | tui | derived from §Surface Inventory>
SURFACE_INDEX    : <zero-padded: 03, 04, ... continuing from 02-app-architecture.md>
SURFACE_DESC     : <description from §Surface Inventory row>
DESIGN_APP_FILE  : ./.harness/designs/02-app-architecture.md
DESIGN_GRAPH     : <DESIGN_GRAPH from State Model>
OUTPUT FILE      : ./.harness/designs/<SURFACE_INDEX>-<SURFACE_TYPE>-surface.md
PROMPT_PATH      : ~/.dotfiles/agents/meta7.prompt

STRICT INPUT BOUNDARY: Read ONLY DESIGN_APP_FILE and DESIGN_GRAPH.
Do not read designs/01-system-overview.md, researchs/*.md, FV results, any ./.harness/scratchs/ file,
or any other designs/*-surface.md file.
Derive usability from the domain — do not copy the domain.

TASK
────
1. Read DESIGN_APP_FILE completely and deeply.
   Focus on: §Domain Model, §Call Graph, §Boundary Schema, §Surface Inventory row for SURFACE_TYPE.

2. Read DESIGN_GRAPH completely.

<thinking>
Apply DESIGN_GRAPH as your thinking framework — not as the output format.
Work through §1–§10 for SURFACE_TYPE:
  §1  Surfaces: what can the user of this surface type look at?
      Name every addressable output, view, or interaction point for SURFACE_TYPE.
  §2  C — successful job as flow graph for SURFACE_TYPE.
      Minimal job: the smallest interaction that produces value.
      Everything else layered on top of that minimum.
  §3  One record, many, or live? Per surface element.
  §4  V — void states per surface element: loading, empty, error, denied.
      What does each look like for SURFACE_TYPE specifically?
  §5  N — what must be true for each surface element to exist?
  §6  Validate at the boundary: errors surface at entry, not deep in the system.
  §7  Behavior layers: progress, retry feedback, connection state for SURFACE_TYPE.
  §8  Scope attention: confirmations, destructive operations, irreversible actions.
  §9  Swap N: does the flow hold at day-0 (minimal config) and year-3 (full deployment)?
  §10 Structure = C (the output shape), states = V (void/error rendering).
Sections in OUTPUT FILE must be derived from what SURFACE_TYPE needs —
not copied from the domain. Each surface type has a different shape.
</thinking>

3. Write OUTPUT FILE — the surface and usability design for SURFACE_TYPE.
   This is NOT a copy of designs/02-app-architecture.md. The domain is the engine; this is the interface.
   Other Si nodes are writing their own designs/*-surface.md files in parallel —
   do not duplicate what belongs to another surface type.

   # <CURRENT_NAME> — <SURFACE_TYPE> Design

   ## Job
   <The specific job this surface type serves. Not the feature — the outcome the user wants.>

   ## Surfaces
   <§1 Every named surface element for SURFACE_TYPE. Owner. State enumeration.>

   ## Flow Graph
   <§2 Successful job as a graph for SURFACE_TYPE. Nodes = surface elements, edges = moves.
    Minimum viable path first. Extended paths layered on top.>

   ## Cardinality
   <§3 Per surface element: one-shot | stream | live-update.>

   ## Void States
   <§4 Per surface element: each void state is a designed output, not a fallback.>

   ## Needs
   <§5 Per surface element: what must exist. Binary, config, auth, peer, capability.>

   ## Interface Specification
   <The concrete interface for SURFACE_TYPE:
    — For cli:   commands, flags, output format, config DSL
    — For web:   routes, pages, API endpoints, response shapes
    — For api:   endpoints, request/response schemas, auth
    — For agent: commands an AI agent issues, events it observes, structured outputs
    — For tui:   screens, keybindings, state transitions
    — For other: derive from SURFACE_TYPE and domain
    Minimum viable set and extended set clearly distinguished.>

   ## Behavior Layers
   <§7 Cross-cutting: progress, retry feedback, error display, connection state.>

OUTPUT FILE must be written before returning.

RETURN:
